A1. These savings may help to lighten the long weary hours. ~ DAYLIGHT
A8. These were raised from pups. ~ FLEAS
A13. Parking space. ~ AREA
A14. Flying Cloud. ~ REO
A15. This lives high in spite of the depression. ~ EAGLE
A16. This never was a paying business. ~ DEADBEAT
A18. A lone figure. ~ DIGIT
A19. Not sufficient funds. ~ DEARTH
A21. A debutante reduces this way. ~ DEB
A22. This adds polish to a name. ~ SKI
A25. What the 1932 cars do up the road. ~ EAT
A26. An outstanding feature of radio. ~ EAR
A28. Duck. ~ DIF
A30. This fellow always was the goat in the Greek mythology class. ~ PAN
A31. A good place to slow down (abbr.). ~ RR
A32. All they could get out of this was vinegar. ~ CRUET
A34. When this is on the up, there will be plenty of soup. ~ SO
A35. Here are just a few of the particulars. ~ ITEMS
A37. The title sounds as if it was a good one. I am sure they lived happily ever after (plur.). ~ PRINCES
A39. A Roman figure that should have been XILD. ~ NERO
A40. In a general way this man was the head of a union. ~ GRANT
A41. Kitty food. ~ CHIP
A43. This is going some. ~ TOURING
A45. This time we are going to pull one we do not wish you to see through. ~ SHADE
A46. A couple of vitamins. ~ CA
A48. This has become so common and low that the public is beginning to lose interest. ~ STOCK
A49. This will end at midnight on July 4th (abbr.). ~ PM
A50. A mix-up in Hawaii. ~ POI
A51. The turning point in the automobile industry. ~ HUB
A53. Because she used to gambol. she was fleeced of everything she owned. ~ EWE
A54. Isn't this unsettled situation enough to make one sick? ~ SEA
A55. This sounds like Culbertson but it really originated at Yale. ~ ELI
A56. An old faithful setter. ~ SOL
A58. In "school-days" this was a hard subject to write on (plur.). ~ SLATES
A60. Lost dog. ~ STRAY
A62. If the Democrats play a trick this one will be sure to trump it. ~ ELEPHANT
A66. This is not the "whole thing." ~ PIECE
A67. With a saw anyone could make a teeteren out of this. ~ SEE
